.spec/decisions.md:1:# Architecture decision log
.spec/decisions.md:15:| ADR-011 | Accepted | Develop packet/SSH/relay core through an SPM macOS harness while proving packetFlow, lifecycle, and memory on the **physical Apple-silicon Mac**. Re-scoped 2026-07-28 by ADR-024: the physical-iPhone row is deferred with iOS and is recorded as a named deferred gap, never as a pass or a waiver. It is re-armed unchanged when iOS resumes. |
.spec/decisions.md:17:| ADR-013 | Gate (release path) | Obtain evidence that local TCP termination + remote `direct-tcpip` is acceptable under Apple's packet-tunnel intended-use guidance. Re-scoped 2026-07-28: Gate A0 is an App Store/App Review release gate and is **not** a gate for the macOS prototype critical path. It stays mandatory before iOS submission and before public macOS distribution claims that depend on it. |
.spec/decisions.md:18:| ADR-014 | Accepted | Select **libssh2** as the primary SSH engine (2026-07-28 owner decision). `ReluxNIOSSH` is retained as recorded comparative evidence and receives no further fork work unless new evidence invalidates libssh2. Selection rests on the ADR-023 M0-viability gate set plus macOS-harness conformance evidence; per-channel window, scale, and memory numbers remain M3 physical-evidence gates and are not asserted by this decision. |
.spec/decisions.md:19:| ADR-015 | Accepted | Select MTU 1500 for the macOS-first M0 baseline; keep 4096 injectable only after end-to-end path proof and reject 8500 as the default because the accepted constrained-buffer matrix produced `EMSGSIZE` sender refusals. The exact buffer, batching, HEV, session, memory, fork, and revalidation bindings are recorded in [`TASK-260715-2jatnd`](../docs/TASK-260715-2jatnd_m0-bridge-hev-decision-adr.md). |
.spec/decisions.md:20:| ADR-016 | Accepted | Set new Apple targets to iOS 18.0 and macOS 15.0; preserve the legacy SwiftPM product at macOS 14.0; require minimum/current CI plus named physical baselines per TASK-260715-3r0993 |
.spec/decisions.md:21:| ADR-017 | Accepted | Use Tuist 4.202.5 pinned exactly by repository-local Mise; generate with an explicit Xcode pin and deterministic graph/project comparison per TASK-260715-3r0993 |
.spec/decisions.md:22:| ADR-018 | Accepted | Pin Sparkle 2.9.4 exactly in `ReluxProxyMac`; require EdDSA-signed payloads plus a signed appcast with pre-extraction verification and no failure expiry; publish only final Developer ID-signed, hardened-runtime, notarized/stapled DMGs; use default stable plus opt-in `prerelease` items on the public HTTPS feed; replace/activate the direct-distribution packet-tunnel system extension only after host relaunch; use higher-build forward rollback because Sparkle 2 has no downgrade path; iOS stays with the App Store |
.spec/decisions.md:23:| ADR-019 | Accepted | Package custom-build C dependencies such as HEV and libssh2 as locally rebuilt static XCFramework SwiftPM `binaryTarget`s behind named native adapter modules; keep reviewable source packages such as ReluxNIOSSH as source dependencies and keep all native dependencies out of `ReluxTunnelCore` proper |
.spec/decisions.md:29:| ADR-024 | Accepted | The prototype physical target is **macOS-only on the current Apple-silicon Mac**. iOS targets, provider, UI, TestFlight, and App Store work are deferred; their identifiers stay defined in the Apple identifier/entitlement matrix so they resume without renaming. Gate P0 is recorded from macOS evidence alone. A Linux CI runner and native Linux relay-asset execution evidence are **not** required for the working-client path; they move to the release path (`TASK-260715-1c4l9v`). Relux Works Apple Development and Developer ID Application **signing identities** are present in the local Keychain. Notarization credentials are **not** yet in a usable Keychain form — see ADR-025. Secrets stay in the Keychain and are never copied to repo, board, logs, or `providerConfiguration`. |
.spec/decisions.md:30:| ADR-025 | Accepted | **Notarization credential custody is Keychain-only.** The only sanctioned runtime form of the App Store Connect notarization credential is a **named `notarytool` Keychain credential profile**; only that profile *name* may be recorded on the board, in the repo, or in CI configuration. The owner-supplied source `.p8` private key currently exists as a mode-0600 file on this host, which does not satisfy the Keychain-only invariant. Before the notarization path is declared ready, `TASK-260728-dveo1o` must (a) store the named profile, (b) verify it against the real notary service, and (c) record an explicit approved disposition for the source file — migration into Keychain-backed custody, retention under a named custodian with documented access control, or secure destruction. The file path, key ID, issuer ID, and key bytes are never echoed, logged, committed, or stored in board resources, and `set -x`/shell history must not capture the `store-credentials` invocation. CI keeps its own separately-scoped credential; the local profile is not exported. |
.spec/decisions.md:31:| ADR-026 | Accepted | **Sparkle key generation is separated from updater integration.** The EdDSA keypair is generated and taken into custody at the up-front ceremony `TASK-260728-q5kjta`, because it depends on nothing; `TASK-260717-ziprhs` records the resulting public-key/custody evidence unattended. `SUPublicEDKey` pinning, CI signing-secret binding, and appcast sign/verify evidence are a distinct downstream task (`TASK-260728-3bj9bk`) blocked by the generated macOS target (`TASK-260715-uyju7n` → `TASK-260717-xempiv`) and the appcast pipeline (`TASK-260717-1mt4e7`). An accepted key ceremony never implies a working self-update signing path. |
.spec/decisions.md:32:| ADR-027 | Accepted | **Deferral is expressed as `blocked` status, not as an unlinked backlog item.** A task deferred by owner decision — Gate A0 research (`TASK-260715-1o3q6l`), further `ReluxNIOSSH` fork work (`TASK-260715-1af33i`), iOS targets (`TASK-260715-33oofa`), physical-iPhone gates (`TASK-260715-1kntdx`) — carries `blocked` status with the evidence packet naming the deciding ADR and the exact input that would resume it. Leaving a deferred task in `backlog` with its blockers removed makes it *schedulable*, which is how prohibited work re-enters an autonomous run. Retained evidence and downstream links are preserved so the branch re-arms unchanged. |
.spec/decisions.md:33:| ADR-028 | Accepted | **One human permission ceremony is one board node, and a human node is never counted as autonomous.** (a) *Ownership.* `TASK-260728-q5kjta` owns Ceremony C1 and is the only up-front human-input node. The evidence tasks it unblocks (`TASK-260715-apc34w`, `TASK-260715-3jloqy`, `TASK-260728-dveo1o`, `TASK-260717-ziprhs`) are agent work that runs unattended with the granted access and keeps its full evidence obligations. Spreading the permission ceremony across several tasks forces the human to re-appear after producer-reviewer routing, which is what the round-2 review rejected. (b) *Labelling.* A task whose completion requires a human action or decision is a **human node** — a scheduler barrier that is never counted in an autonomous total, even when agent work follows inside the same node. Tasks that merely consume access or a decision granted earlier are autonomous and are reported as that node's follow-up. (c) *Later-artifact rule.* An approval that acknowledges a not-yet-existing artifact cannot be pre-granted: Approval A1 (`TASK-260715-9yp8to`), sign-off S1 (`TASK-260715-2ayxqn` AC5), the manual ratification checkpoints (`TASK-260717-1dsqnj`, `TASK-260717-l639qp`, `TASK-260717-2d308k`), and the release-candidate approvals (`TASK-260715-1r48pc`, `TASK-260715-yynqbr`, `TASK-260715-2aessv`) are therefore separate, later interactions, not C1 content. (d) *Resumed evidence amendment, owner-approved 2026-08-10.* One ceremony node does not require one continuous sitting. C1 may combine resumed owner interactions with producer/reviewer work between them when the record states that provenance and does not invent uninterrupted-session boundaries, a historical Always Allow click, or separately timestamped two-factor completion. Acceptance rests on effective prompt-free access for the named signing identities/tools, an authenticated Relux Works portal/team session and confirmed matrix authority, named Keychain-backed notarization and Sparkle custody, and a privacy scan finding zero secret values or secret paths in repository, board resources, run logs, and shell history. Harmless command-name-only history is permitted; secret-bearing command arguments, credential input, key identifiers, account credentials, key bytes, and secret paths remain prohibited. |
.spec/decisions.md:34:| ADR-029 | Accepted | Use the acyclic generated-workspace graph and ownership contract in `docs/TASK-260715-32umrc_generated-project-architecture-adr.md`: Tuist 4.202.5 generates the macOS 15 host/system-extension P0 targets around the local `ReluxTunnel` package; consumer-to-dependency arrows point inward to platform-neutral Core; host owns configuration, provider owns live state, and adapters remain thin. The Go relay is a verified resource input, not linked code. Identifiers, signing lanes, versions, tests, generated-file policy, dependency pins, and legacy SwiftPM coexistence follow the task-scoped ADR. iOS 18 target definitions and fixed identifiers remain deferred under ADR-024/027 and are not generated or built on the macOS-only path. |
