    38	## 2. Trust invariants
    39	
    40	1. Untrusted source is any fork pull request, pull-request head, arbitrary
    41	   branch, mutable tag, downloaded artifact without a verified digest, or
    42	   rerun whose candidate identity differs from its original attempt.
    43	2. Repository source becomes release input only as an exact commit reachable
    44	   from the protected default branch and bound into an immutable candidate
    45	   manifest. A tag is a label, never identity authority.
    46	3. Workflow code that can reach production credentials is loaded only from the
    47	   protected default branch. `pull_request_target` is prohibited for building,
    48	   executing, caching, or uploading pull-request code.
    49	4. Workflow and job permissions default to `contents: read`; every other
    50	   `GITHUB_TOKEN` permission is `none`. A job elevates one named permission only
    51	   for its own call site and only after protected-environment approval.
    52	5. Pull-request jobs have no production environment and receive no certificate,
    53	   private key, certificate password, App Store Connect issuer/key, provisioning
    54	   profile secret, notarization credential, Sparkle private key, feed-origin
    55	   credential, publication token, or privileged runner.
    56	6. Every third-party action is pinned to a reviewed full commit SHA. A mutable
    57	   tag, branch, range, or unreviewed action source fails before execution.
    58	7. Pull-request caches and artifacts are untrusted accelerators. They are never
    59	   release inputs; cache hits are revalidated against source and manifests.
    60	8. Release jobs consume one candidate manifest and exact digests. Reruns may
    61	   reuse that identity but may not resolve a new tag, branch head, dependency,
    62	   cache namespace, or artifact under the old release attempt.
    63	9. Production publication is serialized and feed-last. Cancellation or any
    64	   failed/missing/unknown prerequisite leaves the candidate non-promotable.
    65	10. Absence and read failure are distinct. A missing optional row is `deferred`
    66	    or `not-applicable`; an unreadable or indeterminate required input is
    67	    `unknown` and fails closed.
